erall higher  virtual machine utilization however  openx runs a large number of  applications  written in erlang and airline virtual  machine has its own scheduler threads  which work most efficiently if they run  on the same cpu core  all the time and aren't swapped out or  competing for  cpu with other applications  for scenarios like this kubernetes has a  feature called  cpu manager it provides additional  guarantees of  cpu allocation on top of what kubernetes  resource requests  are already doing while resource  requests  are only used by kubernetes scheduler to  make  informed decisions on where to schedule  pods  the cpu manager enforces cpu allocation  to the pod  continuously prohibiting all other parts  from stepping into  cpu-managed spot territory and  minimizing the number of  contact switches the flip side of cpu  manager is that  pod cannot exceed their requests  and efficiently sandboxed in terms of  cpu capacity  for our airline systems that's exactly  what we wanted  so with auto scaling enabled and our  pods tuned we still needed to tackle  another area of optimization  how do we make the most use of a cluster  that has diverse set of services running  in it  so in a nutshell how do we optimally bin  pack there are two parts to that  challenge  one how do we size our pods so they're  more easily accessible  and then two how do we size our nodes so  that they can accommodate the right  number of pods  we have some control over both  dimensions so we needed to find a sweet  spot  kubernetes scheduler takes several pods  properties into consideration to make  decision on what node plot gets  scheduled  two primary capacity related fields are  resource requests for cpu and memory  kubernetes scheduler does not scale  cluster  up or down but rather works with  available nodes  aiming to fit as many pods as possible  into provisioned capacity and when this  is not possible  and pods are pending cluster autoscaler  provisions  more nodes here's an example of cpu and  memory constraint modes  on the left graph you can see that even  though  only 78 of cpu capacity is  allocated no pods larger than four cpus  can be scheduled  similarly on high cpu node scheduler  node scheduler cannot add any new  parts because the single pod has already  allocated pretty much all memory  capacity  so we ended up with cpu resource  utilization of  just 28 percent  how can this impact inefficiency can  can it be improved you can make all pods  equal size and make sure that each node  can fit  exactly and pods this is not  always ideal as workload capacity  requirements  and ability to scale vertically differ a  lot  at open x we use 2 to the power of n  rule  we standardize spot sizes on 1 2 4  and 16 cores this also  matches gce standard vm sizes and  allows us to move between different vm  sizes without a need to optimize quad  sizes again  while doing so make sure that you  account for space reserved by kubernetes  for demon sets and kernel also don't  forget to pay attention to your  site car and init containers as their  resource requests  might be affecting scheduling decisions  generally speaking you will get best  results of bin packing  when pod requests are small fractions of  cpus  while nodes are relatively large  yet if you're using preemptable virtual  machines  you have to watch for losing large  number of pods  in case of preemption  measuring bin packing efficiency is  quite simple  as you need to aggregate all workload  resource requests  and divide it by total capacity  it can be tracked as a cluster-wide  metric or  notable specific ratio we observed that  auto-scaled clusters with  no pod size optimizations we usually get  around 75 percent of  cpu reserved while clusters with  improved mean pack and efficiency reach  all the way to 95  similar optimizations can be done for  memory dimension  however memory generally requires  significant headroom and usage is  less predictable compared to cpu cores  our memory  reservations are currently measured at  around 50 percent  optimization of bin packing is more than  in more than one dimension is totally  possible  but depending on your workload profile  it may end up  exponentially harder to implement with  every new  dimension that you add we decided to  leave this exercise for the future  joel so early on  when we were doing our cloud cost  modeling yvonne picked out services that  could take advantage of preemptable  virtual machines  and for the delivery stack we assumed  that roughly 70  of our compute could be preemptable now  when we first tested we were discouraged  because it was hard to get the pvm  capacity we needed  but after discussing with the gce team  they encouraged us to try a smaller  sized pvm node  after making that change we were much  more successful in finding the pvm  capacity  and we've even exceeded our initial  target pvm utilization  but it is another factor to consider in  bin packing  so the gke cluster auto scaler allows us  to tap into different pvm pools by  attempting to scale up different node  pools sequentially  that could be node pools of different vm  sizes or using different  compute families  so while we've presented our journey as  a nice linear progression  the reality was a bit more winding with  some back and forth between the  different optimization techniques  that being said the six approaches we've  discussed are all worth looking into  first configure and tune your horizontal  pod auto scalers  to enable the cluster auto scaler three  right size your pod request and or  use the vertical pod auto scaler number  four  use the cpu manager for the applications  that need that level of guarantee  number five optimize your bin packing  and then number six  find the right machine type for your  workload so  thanks to the work that avon and the  team have done we're now able to run our  gke clusters on a very small set of node  pools that scale based on price and  availability  and thanks to the optimized pods the  hpas the right choices and machine sizes  we've improved efficiency by over 45  percent  one of the things that was invaluable to  the success of this project was the  ability to meet with  and ask questions of the different uh  teams whose technology we're using  whether that was jersey and the gke auto  scalers team  the cpu manager team the gce team or  any one of the other teams that we met  with  we're really impressed by their  willingness to uh to work with us and  help us use their technology  we also took advantage of the gcp pso  team to help us in various parts of the  migration  and found their help to be very valuable  as well thank you particularly to will  bebe  so jersey thanks for allowing us to  share some of our story here with you  and uh to the audience good luck with  your own optimization efforts  jersey back to you thank you joel and  yvonne for  sharing your experience with us for  those of you  willing to get on a journey of  optimizing cost of performance on gke  we just published two solution guides  that will get you an advice on  best practices of running cost effective  kubernetes applications  and how to use node auto provisioning  to optimize your resources these guides  are a collective summary of experiences  of openx and many other companies  who shared their stories with us we will  keep updating it  as we add more functionality to google  kubernetes engine  you should also take a look at related  breakout sessions  airing this week on behalf of google  cloud team  i want to thank you for watching
 

